WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.170
I mean, in the beginning,
I just tried to talk myself

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.540
out of starting this
business because it really

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.680
doesn't make sense and in
that time of the world.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.820
But the more I looked into climate change,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.930
the more I understood
what a humongous problem

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:19.733
it is gonna be and I just had no choice.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.230
About two years ago, nobody
was really interested.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.860
They didn't think that it
would be a good business.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.900
They didn't really see the money in it.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.830
That's all changed so rapidly.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.770
Everybody and their grandma
is calling themselves

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.220
a climate investor now,
which is fantastic.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.150
I'm Henrietta Moon.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.710
I'm the CEO and co-founder
of Carbo Culture.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:41.940
And Carbo Culture is
removing atmospheric carbon

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.610
by turning biomass waste
into highly stable carbon

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:47.783
that remains stable for 1,000 years.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.900
Most life on earth is
carbon and it's our fourth

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.140
most abundant element.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.240
So it's not bad per se.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.290
We just have it in the wrong
place in too much scale.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.740
So we need to think of
the most elegant way

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.900
to actually bring down that
carbon from the atmosphere

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.560
and put it to use somewhere
where we can use it better.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.140
This challenge is at the core

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.210
of Carbo Culture's mission.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.890
The Helsinki-based startup
is focused on solving

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.130
the Earth's carbon problem while offering

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.400
a viable business model,
one that didn't even exist

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.630
at the beginning of her career.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.350
I ended up in this crazy program

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.990
that was three-month intense program

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.700
at NASA Ames in California.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.040
And that's where I met my
co-founder, Chris Carstens.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.690
Back then, there was no price on carbon.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:40.570
Emission trading was at
maybe five euros a ton.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.740
And nobody was really
interested in pursuing that

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.350
as a business model.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.150
We got started properly in 2017.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.280
Today, the carbon market is
trading at over 10 times that

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.863
so times have changed.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.090
Henrietta and her team
turned to nature itself

00:01:58.090 --> 00:01:59.940
for inspiration on how to make this model

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.170
both effective and profitable.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.260
Every year, nature draws
down carbon in the form

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.260
of photosynthesis into all our
biomass trees and plantations

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.330
and crops and everything you see.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.010
But naturally, when that
biomass like the trees die,

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.370
they decompose and reenter
the atmosphere again.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.323
And that's the natural carbon cycle.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.270
At Carbo Culture, we
were trying to figure out

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.470
what is the most elegant
way to actually draw down

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.000
some of this carbon?

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.910
And instead of trying to
invent machines to take out

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.660
parts per million from
the atmosphere, we decided

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:36.203
that millions of years of nature
already knows how to do it.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:42.130
So if you see a forest
today and in 500 years

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:43.860
it will essentially be the same size.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:47.760
It's not gonna 10-X its
carbon removal capacity

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.740
or grow 10 times larger.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.650
So we have these solar-powered
allies who use photosynthesis

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.680
and turn this carbon from
the atmosphere into biomass.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.340
And we take that biomass such as nutshells

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.920
or peach pits or tree waste and we put it

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.610
into our patented reactor and we rapidly

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:10.650
take it to a ultra-high
heat where it carbonizes.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.330
It doesn't decompose,
it's not eaten by microbes

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:17.200
or anything, and we can
store it safely in the soil.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.240
By doing this, the carbon
that's almost pure carbon

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.510
remains stable from
1,000 years to let's say

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.133
maybe up to a million years.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.520
Their first facility
resides in California,

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.560
which operates as a proof of concept

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.640
to demonstrate the process.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.590
Basically we put biomass in there.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.790
And we put them inside, we take them

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:44.460
to a ultra-high temperature,
about 1,000 degrees Celsius,

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.010
for a split second.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.890
And in that time, the biomass carbonizes.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:51.760
So it turns into a charcoal-like form.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.220
You can mix it into compost
or put it in the soil

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.830
or use it for water
filtration or just bury it

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.633
in the ground, in a old
mineshaft or something.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:06.490
Our technology is like on a
basic level at the same thing

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:09.880
as turning wood into a carbon is something

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.320
that everybody can do in
their home fireplace, right?

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.650
But now, if you wanna
make it like ultra, ultra,

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:18.910
ultra-efficient and actually
store the carbon away,

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.010
now it gets a little bit more difficult.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.120
But the kinda principle is the same.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.610
Right now, our batch takes about
two hours for a half a ton.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:31.570
So we can do about 500 pounds
per hour let's say of biomass.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.400
Now that they've mastered the concept,

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.610
it's really just a matter of scaling it up

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.290
by a factor of a million or so.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:41.180
If we put a million of these
systems around the world,

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.330
we can actually use part of the
energy in the biomass itself

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:46.000
to power these systems.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.387
And in this way, we're
helping the green transition

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:50.740
of the grid instead of hindering it.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.080
This next scale-up at scale
would remove about 13,000 tons

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.840
of carbon dioxide annually.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.780
So make a few thousand tons of biocarbon.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.743
And that's a big step for us.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:05.353
Though Carbo Culture has recently raised

00:05:05.353 --> 00:05:09.140
$6.2 million in seed
financing, it will take

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:12.680
a lot more funding to get Carbo
Culture's vision to scale.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:15.350
This Herculean effort
will require investments

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:17.890
from both public and private sectors.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:19.940
And now that carbon
capture programs like this

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.520
are beginning to get traction,

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.890
Henrietta welcomes the competition.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.640
If we look at the big picture,

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:29.460
we have a North Star mission
of a gigaton of removal,

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.230
which is one billion tons.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.230
And it's a real insanity target.
It's definitely not easy.

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:38.120
It's light years away
from where we are today.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.270
But for us, it means
that we also need to get

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:41.740
to that kind of a scale.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:45.250
And there's probably gonna be
10 to 100 companies like us

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.620
in maybe a decade from now
that will be trying to get

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.113
to that gargantuan scale that is needed.

